### CONTEXT AND PROBLEM
discovery-service is responsible for pre-warming all applications which
internally involves multiple operations per application:
- Change detection
- Snapshot creation (via API call)
- Rollback execution (via controller)
Currently with a single replica this works but with no coordination.
Under multiple replicas:
- Every replica processes every application — massive duplication.
- Two replicas can detect the same change and write duplicate
  changeLog entries simultaneously.
- Two replicas can trigger snapshot creation for the same app
  at the same time — duplicate or corrupted snapshots.
- Two replicas can execute rollback for the same application
  concurrently — split-brain cluster state.
- No visibility into what is being processed, where, and at what step.
- No recovery if a replica crashes mid-operation.

The solution introduces a distributed coordination layer:
- Leader election for prewarm scheduling — one replica drives the work.
- Per-application distributed lock — one replica processes one app
  at a time regardless of which operation it is.
- Redis Stream as a work queue — applications batched and enqueued,
  consumer workers process them one by one with guaranteed delivery.
- Per-operation state tracking — full observability into what step
  each application is currently at.
- Automatic recovery — crashed replicas release their locks via TTL,
  stale work items are reclaimed and reprocessed.

